{#
  The edge's own probe target, and the only route on this cluster whose whole job is to prove
  that Traefik built a routing table at all.

  #1322: on 2026-09-06 Traefik's boot-time download of the CrowdSec bouncer plugin timed out
  (`context deadline exceeded` against plugins.traefik.io). Traefik keeps running without the
  plugin, so `spec.plugin.bouncer` in the `crowdsec` Middleware resolves to "invalid middleware
  type", and the https entrypoint's middleware chain names that Middleware — which rejects
  EVERY router on the entrypoint. The pod stayed 3/3 Ready with `/ping` answering 200 for 3.5
  hours while every host in the homelab 404'd.

  This route is the startupProbe's target (deployment.yaml.j2). It sits on the `https`
  entrypoint on purpose: that entrypoint's chain in static-config.yaml.j2 carries
  `{{ k8s_namespace }}-crowdsec@kubernetescrd`, so this router is rejected by exactly the
  failure the probe exists to catch, and the probe 404s instead of 200ing. Moving it to
  another entrypoint would leave the probe passing while detecting nothing — ENFORCED by
  ansible/tests/services/test_traefik_edge_selfcheck.py against the RENDERED manifests.

  `ping@internal` is Traefik's own ping handler, reached by name with `kind: TraefikService`
  and no port — the same shape (and the same trap) as the dashboard's `api@internal`; give it
  the default kind and Traefik looks for a k8s Service of that name, finds nothing, and 404s
  silently. It is deliberately NOT a real backend: pointing the probe at an app would couple
  the edge's boot to that app being up, and a startupProbe failure restarts the edge.

  No Authelia. The dashboard route was the obvious existing target and is the wrong one: a 302
  scores as probe success, but it makes an Authelia outage crashloop the ingress edge.

  Gated on traefik_k8s_manage_crowdsec together with the probe, because with the flag false
  there is no plugin, no crowdsec Middleware and no failure mode — see defaults/main.yml.

  The response body is the literal `OK` Traefik's ping handler writes, so nothing about the
  fleet leaks through it.
#}
apiVersion: traefik.io/v1alpha1
kind: IngressRoute
metadata:
  name: traefik-edge-selfcheck
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  entryPoints:
    - https
  routes:
    - kind: Rule
      # Path-only, and deliberately NOT a Host() rule. A kubelet httpGet probe connects to
      # the pod IP, so it sends no TLS SNI, and Traefik answers a matched Host() router whose
      # SNI does not agree with the Host header with `421 Misdirected Request` — measured
      # 2026-09-06 against this very route, which 421'd where a non-matching host 404'd. A
      # probe that can only ever return 421 or 404 never passes, so the edge would crashloop
      # on a change meant to protect it. With no Host matcher there is nothing to disagree.
      #
      # The path is unroutable by any app here and the handler returns the literal `OK`, so
      # matching it on every hostname — including the public ones — costs nothing. Rule
      # priority is rule length, so this only ever wins for requests to this exact path.
      match: "PathPrefix(`/.well-known/traefik-edge-selfcheck`)"
      services:
        # kind + absent port are both load-bearing. See the header.
        - name: ping@internal
          kind: TraefikService
  # An https router with no tls block is a NON-TLS router and is never a candidate for an
  # HTTPS request — see dashboard-ingressroute.yaml.j2. The SAN set is the existing wildcard,
  # so this adds no ACME order.
  tls:
{% if k8s_tls_cert_resolver %}
    certResolver: {{ k8s_tls_cert_resolver }}
{% endif %}
    options:
      name: modern
{% if k8s_tls_cert_resolver %}
    domains:
      - main: "{{ domain }}"
        sans:
          - "*.{{ domain }}"
          - "*.local.{{ domain }}"
{% endif %}
